Anyways, 
<details><summary>Solution</summary>First, here's a copy of the problem so you don't have to keep scrolling up and down:

[asy]
size(200);
import patterns;
pair D=(0,0),C=(1,-1),B=(2.5,-0.2),A=(1,2),AA,BB,CC,DD,P,Q,aux;
add("hatch",hatch());
//AA=new A and etc.
draw(rotate(100,D)*(A--B--C--D--cycle));
AA=rotate(100,D)*A;
BB=rotate(100,D)*D;
CC=rotate(100,D)*C;
DD=rotate(100,D)*B;
aux=midpoint(AA--BB);
draw(BB--DD);
P=midpoint(AA--aux);
aux=midpoint(CC--DD);
Q=midpoint(CC--aux);
draw(AA--CC,dashed);
dot(P);
dot(Q);
fill(DD--BB--CC--cycle,pattern("hatch"));
label(" $A$ ",AA,W);
label(" $B$ ",BB,S);
label(" $C$ ",CC,E);
label(" $D$ ",DD,N);
label(" $P$ ",P,S);
label(" $Q$ ",Q,E);
//Credit to TheMaskedMagician for the diagram
[/asy]

The edges of a regular tetrahedron with vertices  $A ,~ B,~ C$ , and  $D$  each have length one. Find the least possible distance between a pair of points  $P$  and  $Q$ , where  $P$  is on edge  $AB$  and  $Q$  is on edge  $CD$ . $\textbf{(A) }\frac{1}{2}\qquad\textbf{(B) }\frac{3}{4}\qquad\textbf{(C) }\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\qquad\textbf{(D) }\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\qquad\textbf{(E) }\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}$ Well, the longest distance would be  $1,$  if  $P = A, Q = C$  (there are three other ways it could be  $1,$  but let's just look at these two). If we move  $P$  along  $\overline{AB}$  towards  $B$  and move  $Q$  along  $\overline{CD}$  towards  $D,$  we can see that the distance is getting smaller and smaller until  $P,Q$  are midpoints of their respective segments, at which point the length  $PQ$  continues increasing. So, the shortest length  $PQ$  can be found when  $P,Q$  are each midpoints of their segments.

Another way to find this is to look at "edge cases": It's pretty obvious that the shortest distance will occur at one of the extremes. First extreme is just when  $PQ$  is one of the tetrahedron's sides--it's pretty clear this is not the shortest (also all the answer choices are less than  $1$ ). The other edge case would be to check the midpoint, which indeed is the shortest distance with a bit of inspection.

Now that we know  $P,Q$  are midpoints, we actually have to solve the problem. Call the foot of the altitude from  $D$  to  $\triangle ABC$  point  $H$ . Note that this point is also the center of  $\triangle ABC$ . Call the foot of the altitude from  $Q$  to  $\triangle ABC$  point  $I.$  My approach was to find  $PI,QI$  and then use Pythag. Since we know that  $\triangle QIC \sim \triangle DHC$  with ratio  $1:2,$  we can start by finding  $DH$  and  $CH$ . Since  $\triangle ABC$  is equilateral, we have that  $CP = \sqrt{3}/2,$  and thus  $CH = \frac23 \cdot \sqrt{3}/2 = \sqrt{3}/3.$  Then, we have that  $CI = CH/2 = \sqrt{3}/6 \implies PI = \sqrt{3}/2 - \sqrt{3}/6 = \sqrt{3}/3$ . Also, we have that  $CH = \sqrt{3}/3, CD = 1$ , so we can find that  $DH = \sqrt{6}/3 \implies QI = DH/2 = \sqrt{6}/6$ . Since  $QI = \sqrt{6}/6, PI = \sqrt{3}/3,$  we can use Pythag to verify that  $PQ = \boxed{\textbf{(C)} \: \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}}.$</details>

