solution_md:
The last part comes from the fact that  $\triangle{ABC}$  and  $\triangle AEB$  have the same altitude with respect to sides  $\overline{BE}$  and  $\overline{BC}$  because they are coincident and both triangles have the opposite vertex  $A$ .

From there, we can simply relate the lengths of the respective bases to the areas and arrive at the equation
\begin{align*}
\frac{[AEB]}{[ABC]}&=\frac{BE}{BC} &\text{(area + common altitude)}
&=\frac{BD}{BA}&\text{(similar triangles)}
&=\frac{3}{5}
\end{align*}
From which we arrive at the answer of  $\boxed{6}$

