label(" $B$ ",3*dir(20));
label(" $A$ ",3*dir(160));

label(" $d$ ",(0,0));

label(" $a$ ",1.2*dir(90));
label(" $c$ ",1.2*dir(210));
label(" $b$ ",1.2*dir(330));
[/asy]Then, note that PIE yields   $$ |A \cup B\cup C|=|A|+|B|+|C|-|A \cap B|-|B \cap C| -|A \cap C|+|A \cap B \cap C| $$     $$ 102=100+100+101-|A \cap B|-|B \cap C| -|A \cap C|+|A \cap B \cap C| $$     $$ |A \cap B| +|B \cap C| + |A \cap C|=|A \cap B \cap C|+199 $$     $$ (a+d)+(b+d)+(c+d)=d+199 $$     $$ a+b+c+d=199-d. $$   However, note that  $a+b+c+d \leq |A \cup B\cup C|=102$  with equality obtained when none of the sets have completely disjoint elements. Hence  $199-d =a+b+c+d \leq 102$  yields the minimal  $d=\boxed{97}$  obtained at this equality case:

