pair C=(0,0),B=(-2*x,0),A=(-x-s1,s1+s2),D=extension(B,bisectorpoint(A,B,C),A,C),P=foot(D,B,C),Q=foot(D,A,B);
D(MP("A",A,N)--MP("B",B,W)--MP("C",C,E)--cycle);
D(MP("D",D,E)--MP("P",P,S));
D(D--MP("Q",Q,W));
D(rightanglemark(D,Q,A,30));D(rightanglemark(D,P,C,30));D(B--D);
MA(P,B,D,1);
MA(D,B,Q,1.2);
MA(D,C,P,1);
label(" $7$ ",(D+C)/2,E);
label(" $7$ ",(D+B)/2,N,p=fontsize(6pt));
label(" $9$ ",(A+D)/2,E);
label(" $x$ ",(B+P)/2,S);
label(" $x$ ",(C+P)/2,S);
label(" $x$ ",(B+Q)/2,W);
label(" $\sqrt{32+x^2}$ ",(A+Q)/2,W,p=fontsize(8pt));
label(" $\sqrt{49-x^2}$ ",(D+Q)/2,N,p=fontsize(8pt));
[/asy]

Let  $P$  be the foot of the perpendicular bisector. Let  $Q$  be the foot of the altitude from  $D$  to  $AB$ . let  $BP=PC=x$ . By SAS congruency,  $\triangle PBD\cong\triangle PCD$  so  $\angle C$  is congruent to the angles of the bisector. So  $\triangle DBC$  is isosceles. Thus, we have  $BD=7$  as well. We have by AAS congruency,  $\triangle PDB\cong\triangle QDB$  so  $BQ=x$ . Then  $QD=\sqrt{49-x^2}$  and  $AQ=\sqrt{32+x^2}$  all from Pythagorean Theorem. 

Now by the Angle Bisector Theorem, we have \[\frac{BC}{DC}=\frac{AB}{AD}\implies\frac{2x}{7}=\frac{x+\sqrt{32+x^2}{9}.\] Solving for  $x$  yields \begin{align*}18x=7x+7\sqrt{32+x^2}&\implies 11x=7\sqrt{32+x^2}&\implies 121x^2=49(32+x^2)&\implies72x^2=49\times32&\implies x^2=\frac{7^2\cdot2^5}{2^3\cdot3^2}&\implies x=\frac{14}{3}.\end{align*} Note that \[ [ABD]=\frac{(AB)(QD)}{2}=\frac{\sqrt{49-x^2}(x+\sqrt{32+x^2})}{2}.\] We have, \begin{align*}\sqrt{49-x^2}=\sqrt{49-196/9}&=\frac{7\sqrt{5}}{3} \sqrt{32+x^2}=\sqrt{32+196/9}&=\frac{22}{3}.\end{align*} So \[ [ABD]=\frac{(14/3+22/3)(7/3)(\sqrt{5})}{2}=\frac{12(7/3)\sqrt{5}}{2}=14\sqrt{5}\] as desired.

